course: dictionary
lesson: 15552
title: Superstitious
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A superstitious person believes in luck or magic signs. They think some things bring good luck and others bring bad luck, even when there is no proof. «Mia will not walk under a ladder, because she is superstitious.» Mia believes the ladder will bring bad luck.
explain: Look at the clue words around it. Superstitious is an adjective, so it can describe a person, «a superstitious player», or a belief, «a superstitious belief». You can also be superstitious about something, as in «Sam is superstitious about his lucky socks.»
explain: Scientists test beliefs with evidence. If a belief cannot be tested or has no evidence, it is probably superstitious. Ask yourself: is there proof, or is it just a feeling about luck?
fact: Superstitious means believing in luck or magic signs.
fact: Black cats, broken mirrors and Friday the 13th are often said to bring bad luck.
fact: A superstitious belief is based on ideas rather than on facts or science.
fact: In sport, many players are superstitious and wear «lucky» socks or follow the same routine before a match.
fact: In history and stories, superstitious people feared witches and omens.
exercise: «Mia will not walk under a ladder. She is superstitious.» What does superstitious mean? | Very tall and thin | Fond of walking | *Believing in luck or magic signs | Good at maths
exercise: Which of these is a superstitious belief? | *A broken mirror brings seven years of bad luck | Plants need light to grow | Water boils at 100 degrees Celsius at sea level | Magnets attract iron
exercise: Which word describes a belief based on ideas rather than facts or science? | Scientific | Certain | *Superstitious | Measured
test: «Sam wears his lucky socks at every match. He is superstitious about them.» Why might a scientist say his belief is superstitious? | It is about the weather | It is written in a science book | It has been tested many times and proved | *It is based on luck, not on evidence
test: «The superstitious villagers feared the owl on the roof.» What does superstitious describe here? | *Believing in omens and signs | Being very kind | Living near the sea | Being very tidy
exam: Which is the best way to decide whether a belief is superstitious? | Believe it because an older relative says so | *Test it and check the evidence | Wear a charm and hope for the best | Ask a friend if they feel lucky
